279-286:
async function replaceInviteWithCredentials (entu, entityId, invitePropId, session) {
  await setEntity(entu, getObjectId(entityId), [{
    type: 'entu_user', _id: invitePropId,
    uid: session.user.id, email: session.user.email, provider: session.user.provider
  }])
}
```
`session` is derived purely from whoever's OAuth-authenticated bearer key is presented in THIS request's Authorization header (line ~96 `key = ... headers.get('authorization')`) — nothing checks that this session's identity matches any address the invite was originally sent to (there is no address on the token to match against — it holds only `{db, entityId}`). `replaceInviteWithCredentials` writes the CURRENT clicker's `session.user.id/email/provider` onto the invited person's `entu_user` property. This is the code that literally implements 'redemption binds the clicker, not the intended recipient.'

mvox-app's own redeem.ts (`src/lib/invite/redeem.ts`, main) documents and consumes exactly this contract, e.g. lines 6-9: '`AC1 (no auto-provision): this call ALWAYS carries invite=, and presenting any invite param suppresses Entu's auto-create ... Redemption is pure token-possession — the server never compares an email (the token holds none).`' and sends `Authorization: Bearer ${args.sessionToken}` (line 82) — the CALLER'S session, whoever that is.
